        <section id="pair-and-mob-programming"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>Pair and mob programming</h2>
              <p>Click on the images below for a summary of what pair and mob programming are.</p>

              
              <!-- IF 2 --><ul class="l-icon-grid ">
  
  
    
    <!--  -->
      
  
  
    
  
  <li class="c-reveal-block">
    <img class="c-reveal-block__icon js-showhide" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u2_s3_p3_reveal1.svg" alt="Pair programming" width="266" height="266">
    
    <div class="c-reveal-block__desc">
      <h3 aria-hidden="true">Pair programming</h3>
      <p>2 coders work in pairs using one machine. One person, called the driver, writes the code, and the other person, called the observer, reviews it as it’s typed.</p>

    </div>
  </li>
      
  
  
    
  
  <li class="c-reveal-block">
    <img class="c-reveal-block__icon js-showhide" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u2_s3_p3_reveal2.svg" alt="Mob programming" width="266" height="266">
    
    <div class="c-reveal-block__desc">
      <h3 aria-hidden="true">Mob programming</h3>
      <p>The whole team work together in an extended version of pair programming, still on one computer. Along with the driver there will be several observers and diverse roles such as designing, testing and deployment.</p>

    </div>
  </li>
  
</ul>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
