          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Leah:</strong> As we discussed previously, Pair programming is where two people within a team collaborate and work as a pair. So how is this different to Mob programming?</p>
<p>With Mob programming the ‘whole team’ works together involving several people.</p>
<p>In this instance the roles of ‘Driver’ and ‘Navigator[s]’ would rotate between the programmers, and the roles would switch frequently. One person, the Driver would always write the code and the other[s] would observe the code identifying errors and problem solving.</p>
<p>Both Pair and Mob programming practices are especially useful for sharing knowledge.</p>
<p>So, what are the advantages and disadvantages of Pair programming?</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Advantages</p>
<p>Pair programming helps to decrease software errors, it is a less formal review process, and unexpected items would be noticed quicker from Navigator or Observer.</p>
<p>It helps to improve software quality and has a ‘positive impact’ on the source code.</p>
<p>It reduces the ‘risk’ of a project especially if a team member leaves, and experts on specific subjects can share knowledge with team which could help to increase the productivity. It can also help to integrate a new team member who may have problems with the source code as they are unfamiliar with the system.</p>
<p>It also helps with different ability levels as a beginner programmer will increase their productivity when paired with someone more experienced.</p>
<p>Fatigue can decrease productivity therefore swapping ‘Driver and Navigator roles’ can also increase productivity.</p>
<p>It can help with ‘better team cohesion’ reducing the rate at which developers exit the team, and it has been found that pair programming stimulates focus, motivation, and satisfaction, and is enjoyed for the social dimension.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Disadvantages</p>
<p>It has been found that it is more useful for ‘complex projects’ but does not add value for smaller tasks which result in minor bug fixes. It may increase work time and decrease productivity as more work hours are spent in pair programming rather than programming individually.</p>
<p>Some developers need or prefer to work alone, especially when new and needing to build up skills, confidence, and self-assurance from producing their own code. There is also a danger of personality conflict which could in turn decrease productivity.</p>
<p>It can be time-consuming to setup as communication mechanisms, objectives, expectations of approach need to be shared, and continuous feedback is needed. The Project manager or Developer lead would need to manage and identify which adjustments are necessary for the project to be successful.</p>
<p>It may add extra cost to a project as more developer pairs might be needed to meet tighter deadlines.</p>
<p>Good communication processes and mechanisms in pair programming are key, because if there are poor standards or programming practices, and unexpected errors, code could become disorganised and may need a rework.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Mob programming</p>
<p>As we have already discussed ‘Mob programming’ is similar to ‘Pair programming’ except there is one Driver and several Navigators. The main difference is that the team works together, and this could include other tasks for the Navigator roles such as ‘identifying stories’, ‘designing’, ‘testing’, and ‘deployment’ depending on the project.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Advantages</p>
<p>It has been found that Mob programming works well for complex software products.</p>
<p>There is better ‘communication and collaboration’, and the team is self-organised which works with modern Agile practices. There is also more ‘Team alignment’ with an ‘increase in of shared knowledge’, this is especially so where the team do not have a shared understanding of the project.</p>
<p>Teams have found that mob programming can be fun and interactive with an improvement of the learning experience. Mob programming done right can be ‘inclusive’, as it can provide a safe and encouraging environment for beginner programmers or new team members.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Disadvantages</p>
<p>Effects of ‘dominant personalities’ could result in conflict within a larger team, but also ‘Groupthink’ can occur within a team, where the desire for harmony results in poor unchallenged decisions.</p>
<p>Mob programming could end up being ‘less productive’ as it could be difficult to keep focus for the ‘co-pilots’ or ‘navigators’ who aren’t in possession of the keyboard. There could also be concerns of mangers about the difficulty of having the whole team working together for just one element of a project.</p>
<p>Although considered a positive practice Mob programming still needs more validation through research to emphasise its benefits and reduce potential risks.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
